<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cher (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cher is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 365 km long, a left-bank tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a> and flows north and west through Montluçon and Vierzon to join the Loire near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. The Château de Chenonceau spans it on arches. It gave its name to the Cher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cher-river.jpg" alt="Cher (river)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Montulé · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Chenonceau_-_Fa%C3%A7ade_Ouest_et_Tour_des_Marques_reflets.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
